WEBVTT

00:00.210 --> 00:02.220
Instructor: One more thing to talk about with git push

00:02.220 --> 00:06.213
is the dash U option, which you've likely seen.

00:09.060 --> 00:10.770
When you create a new repository,

00:10.770 --> 00:12.360
which I won't, actually.

00:12.360 --> 00:15.510
Well, I guess I kind of have to if I wanna show you.

00:15.510 --> 00:17.040
Eh, okay, I'll delete this one.

00:17.040 --> 00:18.783
But if I make a new repository,

00:19.740 --> 00:21.570
the instructions GitHub gives me,

00:21.570 --> 00:23.670
both of them end with this line,

00:23.670 --> 00:27.120
git push something, but with a dash U.

00:27.120 --> 00:29.460
So what is this dash U option?

00:29.460 --> 00:31.590
Well, it stands for upstream,

00:31.590 --> 00:34.050
which probably doesn't mean a lot.

00:34.050 --> 00:38.400
The upstream of a local branch is kind of like,

00:38.400 --> 00:42.060
think of it as a connection pointing to a remote branch.

00:42.060 --> 00:45.720
So if we have our master branch on our local machine,

00:45.720 --> 00:50.400
normally we want the upstream branch to be origin master

00:50.400 --> 00:52.353
or some master branch on GitHub.

00:53.280 --> 00:54.990
They are connected,

00:54.990 --> 00:58.320
and this enables me to do things like just run git push

00:58.320 --> 01:01.320
without having to specify origin master,

01:01.320 --> 01:05.910
and if I've configured the upstream for my master branch,

01:05.910 --> 01:08.250
then I get to use this shortcut.

01:08.250 --> 01:10.650
So there are other situations where this comes up as well,

01:10.650 --> 01:13.140
like with pulling, and we talked about that.

01:13.140 --> 01:15.120
But again, it's not something that we have to do

01:15.120 --> 01:17.400
in order to push, but it's recommended,

01:17.400 --> 01:19.650
'cause it can make your life easier.

01:19.650 --> 01:23.280
So when we run this, git push -u origin master,

01:23.280 --> 01:27.480
this tells Git that on my computer, locally in my repo,

01:27.480 --> 01:32.480
I want to push up the master branch to origin master,

01:32.700 --> 01:35.070
and I want you to remember that,

01:35.070 --> 01:37.140
I want you to recall in the future,

01:37.140 --> 01:39.600
that my master branch on my computer

01:39.600 --> 01:41.853
is connected to the master branch on origin.

01:42.720 --> 01:45.450
So if I do this right now, let's see.

01:45.450 --> 01:48.270
If I make a new branch on this example we were doing,

01:48.270 --> 01:51.690
let's make a new branch called dogs.

01:51.690 --> 01:52.523
All right?

01:52.523 --> 01:55.260
So this branch, right now, does not exist on GitHub.

01:55.260 --> 01:58.593
If I push it up, git push origin dogs,

02:01.074 --> 02:02.760
you can see it's not here,

02:02.760 --> 02:06.270
but it will make a new dogs branch on GitHub.

02:06.270 --> 02:07.770
There it is.

02:07.770 --> 02:09.030
Okay.

02:09.030 --> 02:11.970
But I did not set the upstream, right?

02:11.970 --> 02:15.690
I did not use that dash U option, but that's okay.

02:15.690 --> 02:18.300
If I want to push up again,

02:18.300 --> 02:21.270
whatever I have on this dogs branch, let's make a change.

02:21.270 --> 02:22.860
Let's make a new file.

02:22.860 --> 02:26.100
dogs.txt, git add dogs, git commit,

02:26.100 --> 02:29.670
just an empty file, create dogs.

02:29.670 --> 02:31.620
If I want to push that up,

02:31.620 --> 02:34.800
I have to do git push origin dogs.

02:34.800 --> 02:37.470
That's totally fine, it's not a big deal.

02:37.470 --> 02:39.990
But normally, I want my dogs branch

02:39.990 --> 02:43.320
to be connected to the GitHub dogs branch, right?

02:43.320 --> 02:47.130
I want this connection right here, dogs pointing to dogs.

02:47.130 --> 02:49.290
My local dogs branch connected

02:49.290 --> 02:53.280
to the GitHub dogs branch on origin, that's standard.

02:53.280 --> 02:55.920
In the previous video, I showed you that you can push

02:55.920 --> 02:58.230
from one branch to something with a different name,

02:58.230 --> 02:59.670
but normally we want them connected,

02:59.670 --> 03:03.450
and if I use this dash U, that will enable it to happen.

03:03.450 --> 03:05.490
But before that, if I just try git push,

03:05.490 --> 03:07.410
you'll see it fails.

03:07.410 --> 03:12.410
It says the current branch dogs has no upstream branch.

03:12.660 --> 03:14.640
So here's one way to do this.

03:14.640 --> 03:16.050
Dash dash set upstream,

03:16.050 --> 03:19.500
or we just use Dash U, which is equivalent.

03:19.500 --> 03:21.330
So I'm gonna do that now.

03:21.330 --> 03:25.020
git push -u origin dogs.

03:25.020 --> 03:28.200
So remember, if we don't use this weird colon syntax

03:28.200 --> 03:30.900
that I showed you in the previous video,

03:30.900 --> 03:33.000
git is going to take our local dogs branch

03:33.000 --> 03:35.280
and push it up to the origin dogs branch.

03:35.280 --> 03:36.840
It uses the same name.

03:36.840 --> 03:40.470
And this dash U says, I want you to remember that.

03:40.470 --> 03:44.190
Set the upstream for dogs to be origin dogs.

03:44.190 --> 03:46.170
And now it shows me this line right here,

03:46.170 --> 03:51.170
branch dogs set up to track remote branch dogs from origin.

03:51.360 --> 03:53.430
So now if I make a change,

03:53.430 --> 03:56.673
let's just make a new file, touch moredogs.

03:58.740 --> 04:03.740
git add moredogs, git commit, create more dogs.

04:04.050 --> 04:07.380
If I do just git push and nothing else,

04:07.380 --> 04:09.720
now we have an upstream set,

04:09.720 --> 04:12.780
and it knows, for this dogs branch that I'm on,

04:12.780 --> 04:14.160
what that upstream is,

04:14.160 --> 04:17.910
and it pushes up to my GitHub dogs branch,

04:17.910 --> 04:20.370
and we see moredogs showing up here.

04:20.370 --> 04:22.290
So that's the use case for dash U.

04:22.290 --> 04:24.690
Normally, you'll use it in this context,

04:24.690 --> 04:27.960
but you could use it in a situation like this

04:27.960 --> 04:31.470
if you wanted to set the upstream to be something other

04:31.470 --> 04:33.720
than the remote branch of the same name.

04:33.720 --> 04:37.920
In other words, if I wanted to set, I'm not gonna do this,

04:37.920 --> 04:42.920
but if I wanted to set the dogs branch locally,

04:42.930 --> 04:46.260
I wanted to push that up to the cats branch

04:46.260 --> 04:48.570
and set that upstream, I can.

04:48.570 --> 04:52.530
So if I ran this, not only would it push my dogs branch

04:52.530 --> 04:55.890
from my machine up to the remote cats branch on GitHub,

04:55.890 --> 04:57.840
it would also say, in the future,

04:57.840 --> 05:00.060
this dogs branch should have an upstream

05:00.060 --> 05:03.390
referring to the origin cats branch.

05:03.390 --> 05:04.770
Normally, you don't want that,

05:04.770 --> 05:06.150
and also, it can be a headache,

05:06.150 --> 05:08.100
and I don't recommend you try this stuff.

05:08.100 --> 05:09.630
You'll run into merging issues

05:09.630 --> 05:11.425
if you're pushing from multiple branches

05:11.425 --> 05:14.850
to the same remote branch, the cats branch,

05:14.850 --> 05:16.380
it's just, you don't wanna do it.

05:16.380 --> 05:17.664
Anyway, it's possible,

05:17.664 --> 05:20.550
but normally, you'll use it in this context.

05:20.550 --> 05:23.640
The very first time you push some branch up,

05:23.640 --> 05:27.510
use that dash U, and the local branch of that name

05:27.510 --> 05:30.900
will have its upstream set to the origin branch of that name

05:30.900 --> 05:32.700
or whatever this remote is called.

05:32.700 --> 05:33.533
All right.
